<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Two months of thirst, frequent urination, tiredness and about 4 kg weight loss in a 35-year-old Hindu monk of South Asian ancestry with a BMI of 24.7 and a random glucose of 246 mg/dL, told of diabetes a year ago and never tested or treated except by diet. Please confirm and classify the diabetes with the fasting tests drawn on the morning of your visit, and advise on treatment that fits his mostly vegetarian monastic diet, his religious fasts and his frequent travel. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within one week. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Is this type 2 diabetes, or type 1, slowly progressive autoimmune or another type, and which tests decide it?</item><item>With symptoms and weight loss, does he need insulin now, or can he start with tablets?</item><item>Which medicines best fit his religious fasts, his mostly vegetarian diet and long periods of travel?</item><item>How should he keep his fasts safely, and when should he break a fast?</item><item>How should the kidneys be protected, given any albuminuria and his raised blood pressure?</item><item>What targets and monitoring do you advise, including lipids at 35 with South Asian ancestry?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Swami Vivekananda, 35, a Hindu monk, is seen at the request of Dr. Thornbury-Mensah for two months of thirst, nocturia, tiredness and weight loss. He was told of diabetes a year ago and has managed it only by diet. He eats mostly vegetarian meals at his monastery, with fish at some, and keeps fasts twice a month and at major festivals; a few days ago a night-long waterless fast left him light-headed and very thirsty. He calls diabetes hereditary in his family. He prefers tablets to injections but will accept what is needed. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>He was born Narendranath (Narendra Nath) Datta in an affluent family in Calcutta (Kolkata) on 12 January 1863. His father, Vishwanath Datta, was a successful attorney; his mother was Bhuvaneshwari Devi. [D]</item><item>He met Sri Ramakrishna at Dakshineswar in November 1881 and became his disciple; in 1887 he and his brother disciples took the formal vows of sannyasa and new monastic names. [D]</item><item>He left Bombay for America on 31 May 1893 and spoke at the World&apos;s Parliament of Religions in Chicago in September 1893, which made him famous; he then spent nearly three and a half years teaching Vedanta, mostly in the eastern United States and London, and returned to India in January 1897. [D]</item><item>On 1 May 1897, at a meeting of the monastic and lay devotees of Sri Ramakrishna in Calcutta, he founded the Ramakrishna Mission Association; in February 1898 the monastery moved to a garden house at Belur on the west bank of the Ganga, and land for the Belur Math was bought in early 1898. [D]</item><item>In March and April 1897, soon after his return to India, he wrote that he had diabetes. On 16 March he wrote: &quot;The work has broken me down completely, and I have got &apos;diabetes&apos;, an incurable disease&quot;; on 26 March: &quot;The result of this steady work in the West and the tremendous work of a month in India upon the Bengalee constitution is &apos;diabetes&apos;. It is a hereditary foe,&quot; adding that &quot;Eating only meat and drinking no water seems to be the only way to prolong life&quot;; and on 7 April that he had been &quot;attacked by &apos;Diabetes&apos;, a fell disease&quot; and had gone to Darjeeling, cool and &quot;very good for the disease,&quot; where the doctors did not want him to move about. A biography says he went to Darjeeling on the advice of physicians. [D]</item><item>On 29 May 1897, from Almora, he wrote to a physician disciple that he was taking the medicines sent to him, riding morning and evening and feeling very strong, but that &quot;I never in my life could sleep as soon as I got into bed. I must toss for at least two hours,&quot; that he felt hot after the evening meal, that &quot;There has not been any thirst even though the days are fearfully hot,&quot; and that he was afraid of &quot;getting fat on a too much milk diet.&quot; [D]</item></list><paragraph>10 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

