    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Type 2 diabetes (E11.65). Question 1: diabetes is confirmed by two abnormal results (A1c 9.4 percent, fasting glucose 172 mg/dL). GAD65 antibodies are negative and his fasting C-peptide is preserved, so this is type 2 diabetes; slowly progressive autoimmune diabetes is unlikely, though antibodies should be rechecked if he loses weight again or needs rapidly escalating treatment. Monogenic diabetes is unlikely without a clear three-generation pattern. In South Asians type 2 diabetes appears earlier and at lower BMI, with more insulin resistance for a given weight, which is why ADA screens Asian Americans from a BMI of 23. Question 2: insulin is not needed now. He has no ketosis, his own insulin secretion is good and the A1c is below 10 percent; his weight loss reflects glucose spilling into the urine and should stop with treatment. [S]</item><item>Moderately increased albuminuria, first sample (R80.9). Question 5: 46 mg/g on one sample, which hyperglycemia can raise; it needs two of three abnormal samples over three to six months before diabetic kidney disease is diagnosed. [S]</item><item>Raised blood pressure (R03.0). Clinic and home readings of about 136/86: with diabetes and albuminuria the target is below 130/80. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Thornbury-Mensah&apos;s intake note: random glucose 246 mg/dL; urine glucose 3+ without ketones; creatinine 0.96, eGFR 106; BMI 24.7; blood pressure 138/86. This morning, fasting: A1c 9.4 percent; plasma glucose 172 mg/dL; GAD65 antibodies 1.2 IU/mL (negative); C-peptide 2.6 ng/mL; urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio 46 mg/g; total cholesterol 214, HDL 36, triglycerides 228, LDL 132 mg/dL; ALT 38 U/L (FIB-4 0.6); TSH 1.8; vitamin B12 312 pg/mL. Home blood pressure average 136/86. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
