    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Question 3: start metformin extended-release with the evening meal, 500 mg for a week, then 1000 mg, rising to 2000 mg if tolerated; extended-release taken with the meal that ends a fast suits his fasts. A second tablet now would also be reasonable at this A1c, but starting with metformin and diet and reviewing in about five weeks is safe and is his preference. Avoid sulfonylureas, which cause hypoglycemia during fasts. A GLP-1 receptor agonist is not the first choice for a man who has just lost weight unintentionally and prefers tablets. [S]</item><item>Once albuminuria is confirmed, add an SGLT2 inhibitor for kidney and heart protection, with clear rules: skip it on waterless fast days and stop it when unable to eat or drink. [S]</item><item>Question 4: by IDF-DAR risk stratification, adapted from Ramadan to his fasts, his risk is moderate while glucose is high and low on metformin once it is steady. Fasts with fruit, milk and water are reasonable now; avoid waterless fasts until glucose is steady below 180 mg/dL, and then keep them short and drink well before and after; check glucose at midday and before breaking a fast; break it if glucose is below 70 or above 300 mg/dL, or if he is dizzy, confused or vomiting. [S]</item><item>Medical nutrition therapy with a dietitian: a mostly vegetarian plan built on his monastery meals (dal, vegetables, whole grains and yogurt, with smaller portions of rice and sweets), respecting his choices; healthy plant-based diets are associated with better cardiometabolic risk in South Asians. [S]</item><item>Question 5: if home blood pressure stays at or above 130/80, an ARB (losartan) rather than an ACE inhibitor; potassium and creatinine one to two weeks after starting; repeat the urine albumin twice over three to six months. [S]</item><item>Question 6: A1c target below 7 percent, A1c every three months until at target; retinal photographs and a foot examination at diagnosis; vitamin B12 periodically on metformin; a moderate-intensity statin is reasonable at 35 with diabetes, South Asian ancestry, albuminuria and raised blood pressure; hepatitis B and pneumococcal vaccines. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Thornbury-Mensah the same day. The results and the reasons for each recommendation were explained to Swami Vivekananda, including why his fasts and his diet are his to decide and how the plan adapts to them. He decides who else receives this note. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Endocrinology review in three months, or sooner if glucose stays above 250 mg/dL, ketones appear or he loses weight again; Dr. Thornbury-Mensah continues primary care and shares the monitoring. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
